WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning Hank it’s Tuesday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.080
So in addition to our

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.700
long running collaboration here
on vlogbrothers, we have a

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.419
podcast Dear Hank &amp; John
where we answer listener

00:00:07.419 --> 00:00:09.940
questions, provide dubious
advice, and give people all

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.879
the week’s news from
both Mars and AFC Wimbledon.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.669
Making the podcast is one
of the highlights of my week

00:00:14.669 --> 00:00:17.289
because it’s two uninterrupted
hours with my brother.

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:21.480
And it has always been so
joyful until 46 days ago when

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.410
someone asked a question that
burrowed into my brain and

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.050
has refused to let me go.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.180
A listener named Sarah wrote in

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.580
to say that 8 years earlier when
she was diagnosed with asthma,

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:35.630
her doctor, a licensed physician,
asked her how often she sneezed.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.100
Sarah responded that she didn’t
sneeze that much, just the

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.100
normal amount.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.880
And then the
doctor said, “sneezing is not normal.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.550
I never sneeze.”

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.020
And then Sarah
went on to say that for the last 8 years

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:50.340
every time she sneezes she thinks
about this doctor who told her that

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.090
sneezing is not normal.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:54.420
And now,
having heard this story, every time

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.680
I sneeze I also think about this doctor.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.170
In this respect I am not alone,

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:03.440
because since the podcast first aired
I have heard from hundreds of people

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.140
who say that every time they
sneeze they think sneezing is

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:07.790
not normal, I never sneeze.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:08.790
I suppose part of what makes

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.160
this thing so sticky in my
mind is trying to fathom

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.350
what this doctor thinks
when they do sneeze.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.490
Right like if you believe
the sentence, “I never sneeze”

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.510
how do you make sense
of the times when you do

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.510
sneeze?

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:20.510
Do you pass it
off as a fluke?

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:21.510
Do you think

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.960
to yourself, “that wasn’t a real
sneeze that was more of a

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.820
sneeze adjacent phenomenon.”

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:25.860
Or do you see your sneezing

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:29.390
as some sort of moral weakness
or character failing to be hidden

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.790
from the public?

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.000
And part of the
reason I can’t stop thinking

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.310
about this Hank is that you named
this person Dr. NeverSneezer

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.710
Scrooge which is just such a
perfect joke.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:39.710
How could I ever

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.180
let that leave my brain.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:42.180
But I think there’s something

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.820
else going on too, which is
that ridiculous and absurd

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.000
and outrageous statements just
stay with me.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.000
They penetrate in a

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.620
way that carefully considered
nuance statements don’t.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.270
Like if this doctor had said, “sneezing can be
one symptom of seasonal allergies

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:59.810
do you also experience a stuffy nose”
I wouldn’t know about this doctor

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.690
and I certainly wouldn’t think
about them every time I sneeze.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.780
And in our attention economy,
where the mere act of capturing

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:10.630
attention is on some level valuable,
saying “I never sneeze” is certainly

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:14.640
going to capture more attention
than like being a good doctor.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.459
Similarly, people who say
outrageous stuff online can

00:02:17.459 --> 00:02:19.400
capture a lot of attention.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.850
Like if you say Stalin was

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.930
underrated or that it isn’t
necessary to wash your lower

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.700
legs or that Grease 2 is the
canonical Grease movie you

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.450
will get lodged inside many
people’s minds.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.450
Now you won’t

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:33.780
be thought of in primarily a
positive way but still there is

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.480
value to be found in saying the
stupidest possible things

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.599
in the loudest possible way.

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:41.290
Which just to state the obvious,

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:44.430
is not a good way to organize
an attention economy.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:49.120
But that, to use doctor terms,
is a diagnosis and not a treatment.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.790
Because I don’t know the treatment.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.660
How am I to free myself from thinking

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.460
“sneezing is not normal, I never sneeze,”

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:55.460
every time I sneeze?

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.460
Did I

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.350
make the world worse in
albeit a very minor way by

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:02.130
sharing Sarah’s story of her
ridiculous sneeze denying physician?

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.430
Am I making the world worse now
by sharing that story with more

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.350
people via a Youtube video?

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:08.580
Those aren’t rhetorical questions.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.470
I don’t know what to do about the fact that
what grabs my attention in a crowded

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:16.930
information landscape is always going
to be what looks scary or different

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.130
or outrageous or ridiculous.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:20.350
I do realize that this is a lot of

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.849
weight to put on “sneezing is not
normal I never sneeze” but I

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:26.879
genuinely dislike the fact that
some random doctor saying

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:29.510
something ridiculous has
reshaped my sneezing

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.319
experience forever.

00:03:31.319 --> 00:03:34.570
Until quite recently I
thought about the internet and IRL as

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:37.379
being fundamentally separate spaces.

00:03:37.379 --> 00:03:39.100
Like when I was young, what I liked

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.069
about the internet was precisely
that it was not IRL.

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:44.069
But in truth

00:03:44.069 --> 00:03:46.990
there is no bright line between
real life and cyberspace and the

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:52.000
outrageous ridiculousnesses we
encounter whether online or off,

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.390
end up shaping the people we
are and the world we share.

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.190
And I don’t know what to
do about that.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:03:59.190
Hank,

00:03:59.190 --> 00:03:59.690
I’ll see you on Friday.

